    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Coastal erosion is an important product of physical weathering by the ocean, accelerated by strong\r\nstorm events. Different morphological aspects of specific areas of interest play an important role in\r\nhow a storm will affect the coastline. Swell size, direction, and surge are three storm characteristics\r\nthat may be determining factors in coastline erosion rates. Understanding these characteristics in\r\nspecific regions would allow for better erosion forecasts for future storms. This research is primarily\r\na case study focused on one storm event in one region, specifically the erosion to a section of coastline\r\nin Santa Cruz, California caused by a storm on January 5th, 2023. I obtained wave data from an\r\nNDBC buoy during this storm and created a nested wave model in SWAN and examined wave height,\r\npower, and incident angle along the coastline. I compared these results to those of rotated storm\r\nspectra, average summer and winter seasonal wave events, and varied surge heights. I examined\r\nrates of erosion along this section of coastline in relation to the physical characteristics of the storm\r\nand each scenario to determine correlations. I found swell direction, storm surge, and the angle of\r\nthe shoreline are key features in predicting erosion. Findings from this study can be replicated for\r\nother areas of interest to evaluate potential erosion from future storms.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
